What is Alias in Route 53?

Alias is an AWS-specific DNS feature that lets you point your domain directly to a supported AWS resource.
We use Alias mainly when we want our domain to point directly to an AWS resource, especially when using the root domain.
Remember this for interview
Alias is used in Route 53 to point a domain directly to supported AWS resources, such as an ALB or CloudFront,
without manually managing their IP addresses. It is especially useful for the root domain because CNAME cannot be used at the root.
CNAME = point a subdomain to another DNS name.
Alias = point a Route 53 record directly to a supported AWS resource, including when using the root domain.
For example, your ALB has an AWS DNS name:

my-alb-123.ap-south-1.elb.amazonaws.com

Instead of manually managing the ALB's IP addresses, you can do:

hi.com
   ↓
Route 53
   ↓
A Record + Alias ON
   ↓
Application Load Balancer
   ↓
EC2

without Alias, you can use the ALB's DNS name with CNAME, but not for the root domain.
Example:

www.myapp.com
      ↓
CNAME
      ↓
my-alb-123.ap-south-1.elb.amazonaws.com

Here:

www.myapp.com → subdomain ✅
myapp.com → root/apex domain ❌ with CNAME

For the root domain, use Alias:

myapp.com
   ↓
A record + Alias
   ↓
ALB







Why Alias instead of CNAME?
For the root domain:
myapp.com
you generally use A Alias → ALB.
For a subdomain:
www.myapp.com
you can use either A Alias → ALB or a CNAME → ALB DNS name.
So the important idea is:
ALB gives you an AWS DNS name. Route 53 lets your own domain name point to that ALB.



Alias can point to particular supported AWS resources, not just a Load Balancer.
What about EC2?
For a normal EC2 instance, you generally don't use an Alias record directly to the EC2 instance.
So when I said "Alias = AWS resource", I meant supported AWS resources, not every AWS resource.
Easy interview answer:
"A Route 53 Alias record can point a domain to supported AWS resources such as an ALB, NLB, CloudFront distribution, or S3 website endpoint."
When Alias is ON, CNAME is not used. That's because an Alias record is used with certain record types, typically A or AAAA.
CNAME works for a subdomain.


